<h2 style="text-align: center;">All Happy Classrooms<br />
<em><strong>by Daniel Story</strong></em></h2>
<p>Once a week, I sit in a circle with 20 students and participate in a discussion about the love lives of the rich and powerful.</p>
<p>I’m teaching a 10-week undergraduate philosophy seminar on love and care, and we’re reading <em>Anna Karenina</em> alongside several hundred pages of philosophical texts. On <em>Karenina </em>days, I listen and occasionally chime in. We talk about whether the adulterous Oblonsky loves his wife, how the teetotaling Levin can be friends with dissolute aristocrats, whether Anna’s husband drove her away with aloofness. We discuss to what extent the philosophers’ tidy accounts of love grip Tolstoy’s muddy intricacies. We imagine Vronsky on the apps. The conversation winds along circuitous paths.</p>
<p>This shouldn’t really be possible. We’ve all seen the headlines. Students <a href="https://slate.com/human-interest/2024/02/literacy-crisis-reading-comprehension-college.html">can’t read books</a> anymore. Students <a href="https://fortune.com/2026/01/09/gen-z-college-students-struggling-to-read-professors-forced-to-rethink-standards-warn-of-anxiety-lack-of-workplace-prepardness/">can’t read </a><a href="https://fortune.com/2026/01/09/gen-z-college-students-struggling-to-read-professors-forced-to-rethink-standards-warn-of-anxiety-lack-of-workplace-prepardness/"><em>sentences</em></a> anymore. Diagnoses vary, but everyone agrees that the central culprit is <a href="https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/2024/11/the-elite-college-students-who-cant-read-books/679945/">technology</a>: “Reading books, even for pleasure, can’t compete with TikTok, Instagram, YouTube.”</p>
<p>Yet most of my students are reading well over one hundred pages per week. I know they are reading because of the character of our discussions, which are filled with interpretive disagreement, fixation on details, forgetfulness that brings us to our books. There is much speculation about how things will turn out. I know they are reading (and when they are not) because of the in-class writing assignments I give them. I know because I watch bookmarks and spine creases advance each week. I know because my colleagues report that their students are reading Tolstoy during class breaks. I know because I see my students read beside me on picnic blankets in the reading sessions I host on the campus lawn.</p>
<p>Either the headlines are misleading, or there’s something exceptional happening in this class. I think it’s both.</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">◊   ◊   ◊</p>
<p>If you want to learn something about yourself, tell your friends you’re going to teach a seminar on love and watch their eyebrows.</p>
<p>I do feel ridiculous teaching this topic, like Alcibiades stumbling impetuously into a dialogue he’s too crude to appreciate. Maybe I’m not unusual in this way. “Philosophers, although they are supposed to be lovers of a sort, tend to be all thumbs when it comes to handling love,” <a href="http://books.google.com/books?vid=ISBN9780700604791">Annette Baier once wrote</a>. “The ones who have told us most or most insightful things about love are poets and novelists.”</p>
<p>The idea to teach Tolstoy came from someone I love and respect. It was a rainy Sunday morning, and I was asking in a desultory way about what kind of non-philosophical works I should include in my seminar, which started the following week. My companion immediately suggested <em>Anna Karenina</em>. I immediately told her the suggestion felt ambitious and right. “You might start a lifelong practice of reading in them.” We talked smilingly in the pleasure of sudden concord.</p>
<p>It remains to be seen whether anyone will develop a lifelong practice. But I’m already glad for the suggestion. Several of the philosophers on my syllabus refer to <em>Anna Karenina</em>. Tolstoy depicts the interiority of love in exquisite detail. The book has proven to be a fecund source of conversation.</p>
<p>On top of this, I’m glad the idea did not originate with me. I feel there’s something precious about taking in something from someone else’s mind. To share an idea is to draw it from the abstract realm and incarnate it as connective tissue. Ideas acquire new significance within the relational realm. They become media of recognition and objects of responsibility. They give expression to togetherness, even love. Shared things are important.</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">◊   ◊   ◊</p>
<p>My students are coming of age as philosophers in a time when it is increasingly possible to go it alone. The lone philosopher has always been a myth. Yet if philosophy means things like understanding compatibilism and publishing in <em>Ethics</em>, this will not always be true; autodidactic vibe-philosophizing is coming.</p>
<p>For me, though, philosophy means something different. For me, philosophy is reflective inquiry about human experience that connects us in deep intellectual ways to both ourselves and other human beings. If I were the only person, I would not have the constitution for philosophy. I am too oriented in my philosophical practice towards relationships that are suffused with the connective tissue of philosophical thought.</p>
<p>One of my pedagogical aims is to share this orientation with my students. I want them to experience its pleasures and virtues. I want to bring them into a relational realm where, through their interpersonal responsibilities, they are (to modify an expression <a href="http://books.google.com/books?vid=ISBN9780195180992">from Virginia Held</a>) “compelled to congeal into any entity capable of intellectual agency.”</p>
<p>Concretely, in my class this involves a shared intention to investigate love through meaningful discussion of a Big Book. I believed we could do this, but I wasn’t sure. The pundits don’t seem to think students, especially “non-elite” ones, can read something like <em>Anna Karenina</em>. I invited my students to help me prove them wrong.</p>
<p>This framing has been successful. Many of my students want to be part of our experiment. They want to avoid letting me and their classmates down. To do this, they have to read. More than that, they have to congeal into agents with distinct intellectual perspectives from which they can contribute. Without this, our shared activity will not materialize. We will fail.</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">◊   ◊   ◊</p>
<p>Nel Noddings <a href="http://books.google.com/books?vid=ISBN9780520275706">tells us</a> that “the primary aim of every educational institution and of every educational effort must be the maintenance and enhancement of caring.” Teaching is about training the intellect through a cooperative dialogical practice that nurtures an ethical ideal. This ideal is communicated through the teacher’s engrossment in and care for her student and through the admirable image of the student that the teacher reflects back to him. “The teacher works with the student. He becomes her apprentice and gradually assumes greater responsibility in the tasks they undertake.” This shared activity “produces both joy in the relation and increasing competence in the cared-for.” It prepares the student for mature moral and intellectual life.</p>
<p>The shared activity in my classroom requires us to concern ourselves with one another’s actions. (Did enough of us get through the reading to sustain a two-hour discussion?) It renders us accountable to one another. (Is repeatedly passing the buck when called upon to summarize plot points justifiable?) It binds us through increasingly fibrous connective tissue. (What do we think of Anna this week?) And it contains incentives that align with the aims of pedagogy. Our shared activity gives me reason to be engrossed in my students’ intellectual perspectives, to encourage them when they struggle, and to affirm their successes.</p>
<p>There is another way in which our shared activity nurtures an ethical ideal. In the <a href="https://doi.org/10.2307/2026358">words of Martha Nussbaum</a>, the richness of Tolstoy’s characters compels us to “actively care for their particularity.” As we become immersed in Tolstoy’s world, “we strain to be people on whom none of their subtleties are lost, in intellect and feeling.” We practice a kind of sustained and detailed attention that resembles the attention required for care. We mature ethically.</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">◊   ◊   ◊</p>
<p><em>Anna Karenina </em>is a reflection on how love and care are constrained by the cultural systems in which they find expression. Anna and Vronsky’s love is born passionately within an illicit affair, and is later undone by elements&#8212;stigma, jealousy, paranoia&#8212;of that same social script. Levin’s capacity to care for his dying brother is compromised by his socialized inability to see Nikolai as a locus of needs.</p>
<p>As teachers, our ability to nurture students’ intellectual and moral faculties is constrained by cultural systems, too. Educational institutions are organized around efficiency and profit. The kinds of relations I have with the students in my seminar are hardly possible in a class of 30 or 300. They are hardly possible for my lecturer colleagues who are paid less to teach twice as much as I do.</p>
<p>Pedagogical care is also constrained by narratives about our students’ supposed degradation, which reflect back to them an unadmirable image of themselves. Consider how the Friends of Attention, an “attention activist” group, write about people in their book, <a href="http://books.google.com/books?vid=ISBN9798217086160"><em>Attensity</em></a>: “Look around. See anything odd? Like, everyone staring at a device, and nobody talking? Nobody <em>actually seeing </em>each other? Or, for that matter, the <em>world</em>? Or <em>anything </em>but a stream of ‘content’ that is continuously monetizing their consciousness? Hmm.”</p>
<p>Narratives like this are sensational. They can be particularly discouraging to students outside elite institutions since they are often framed by <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2025/09/05/podcasts/hardfork-education-alpha-school.html">saying something like</a> “even in the most elite circles” people can’t read books anymore. This implies that non-elite students are somehow inherently less equipped for reading. I don’t believe this. I suspect that the grit required to be a <em>non</em>-elite student–a transfer, a first-gen, a nontraditional–means non-elite students are often <em>better </em>equipped for reading than students enveloped in privilege.</p>
<p>The framing also stings. I am a reader who started out at Bluegrass Community and Technical College and has never been in a particularly elite institution. I feel protective of and indignant for my students. They can be readers, just like me.</p>
<p>All this is to say that the narratives feel wrongheaded for multiple reasons. Yet wrongheaded cultural narratives have the power to shape reality and our relations to one another. They are insidious. If we want to nurture our students, we should reject them.</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">◊   ◊   ◊</p>
<p>Anecdotes are not statistics. I have not provided reason to think that students are generally willing and able to read long texts. I don’t doubt most students’ attentional habits and preferences have changed such that they read less than they used to. What I doubt is that this means we should assign less reading.</p>
<p>Teaching ought to be a care relation. Care is not a matter of conforming to principles or generalizations. Care is a matter of attending to the particular needs of the cared-for. Possibly, most students either can’t read over 100 pages per week or wouldn’t benefit from it. But that doesn’t mean that my students, or your students, can’t or wouldn’t. We have a responsibility to find out by attending to our students rather than pundits.</p>
<p>My own view is that the technocultural dynamics undermining reading provide reason to assign my students <em>more</em> reading, not less. I know my students can read. And I believe Nussbaum is right that novels are an important means of developing moral attention. To me, this suggests that it would be good to nurture an ethical ideal by providing my students with “attentional sanctuaries” (one worthwhile idea from <em>Attensity</em>) wherein they can partake in immersive textual experiences that, due to the technological landscape, they are increasingly unlikely to choose of their own volition.</p>
<p>There is no one-size-fits-all pedagogical method. But my experience, as well as that of others such as <a href="https://dailynous.com/2025/12/22/when-your-students-write-the-book-of-your-course-guest-post/">David Kishik</a>, seems to indicate that introducing shared agency into the classroom through a shared intention with uncertain prospects and genuine buy-in from the teacher is a promising strategy for both motivating serious reading and fostering communities bound by the connective tissue of philosophical thought. Communities like this awaken students to the pleasures and virtues of a relational orientation in philosophical practice. Sharing this orientation, in whatever particular way each of us can, to me seems to be a caring pedagogical response to a technological world where it is increasingly possible to go it alone.</p><p>The post <a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/02/24/all-happy-classrooms-guest-post/">All Happy Classrooms (guest post)</a> first appeared on <a href="https://dailynous.com">Daily Nous</a>.</p>